    "body": "© Iakov Filimonov\nBy Gavin van Marle\n20 November 2026\nContainer spot freight rates on the transpacific trades finally began to show more normal slack-season behaviour this week, with significant declines from Asia to both the US west and east coasts.\nHowever, spot rates on the Asia-Europe trade moved in the opposite direction, with Asia-Mediterranean rates showing a small spike after three weeks of flatlining.\nThis week’s World Container Index (WCI) from Drewry recorded spot rates on its Shanghai-Genoa leg up 6% over the previous seven days, to finish at $4,436 per 40ft, while Shanghai-Rotterdam was unchanged, at $3,496 per 40ft.\nEuropean forwarders expressed some surprise at the strength of the Asia-Mediterranean pricing, which appeared slightly divorced from demand and supply fundamentals.\n“Yes, we did see a big hike mid-November on rates for Asia to Med, but we’ve not seen a big jump in demand, or issues with getting space, so it’s a bit of a head-scratcher, especially with more Med services going through Suez now,” one told The Loadstar .\nHe added that further hikes had been notified for the first half of December, through a mixture of new FAK levels – broadly in the range of $4,500 per 40ft to North Europe and $5,500 to the Med – as well as pre-Chinese New Year peak season surcharges (PSS), currently ranging from $1,000-$1,600 per 40ft, depending on carrier and destination, due to be implemented on 1 December.\n“It depends on the carrier, but we are seeing a mix of base rate increases and surcharge add-ons, and we’re seeing the same for North Europe for the first half of December.\n“But the rate increase doesn’t seem to match demand levels, so we are not sure it will all stick. It’s also quite early to get December levels, so it feels like a punt from the carriers at this stage,” he added.\nOn the Transpacific, rates continued their downward trend on the WCI, for the seventh consecutive week, Drewry noted, its Shanghai-New York rate declining 9% week on week, to finish at $8,194 per 40ft, while the Shanghai-Los Angeles leg was down 5%, to $7,026 per 40ft.\nToday’s levels are the lowest Asia-US east coast spot rates in 15 weeks, and the lowest to the west coast for 12 weeks.\nAccording to Drewry’s Container Capacity Insight, 10 blank sailings have been announced for next week, compared with four this week, although the analyst said that, “despite these capacity reductions, Drewry expects freight rates to decrease further next week”.\nHowever, US west coast forwarder Freight Right warned shippers that steep pricing falls were unlikely in the short term.\n“Looking ahead to the remainder of November and early December, ocean freight rates are expected to remain largely stable,” it said.\n“Importers should not anticipate dramatic rate drops, as carriers are strategically maintaining baseline pricing in preparation for the January shipping cycle.\n“However, because carriers still have scheduled weekly sailings, and want to avoid moving empty vessels, shippers may see targeted, vessel-specific promotional rates emerge in early December,” it added.\nHowever, there is also more capacity entering the trade over the next two-to-three weeks.\nZim is set to launch a new premium transpacific liner service between China and the US on 5 December. The ZX3 will deploy five ships with an average capacity of 4,300 teu on a Xiamen-Shanghai-Los Angeles rotation, with a 12-day transit time.\nMeanwhile, MSC notified shippers today it would reinstate its transpacific Mustang service from Asia to the Pacific north-west coast, which it suspended at the end of 2024. It will feature a port rotation of Yantian-Ningbo-Busan-Seattle-Vancouver, and the first sailing will be the departure of the 6,000 teu GSL Dorothea from Yantian on 13 December.\nIt has not revealed which other vessels will be deployed on the string, but previous iterations of the Mustang service have deployed between five and seven vessels.",
